  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Arizona total $29.3B in FY2026 to date. The contracting environment shows active volume: there are 18,502 awards so far and the average award size is $1.6M. Obligations are up 25.5% year over year. Obligations rose from $23.3B in FY25 to $29.3B in FY26, after several earlier-year swings between $15.3B and $19.8B. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer with $20.9B in obligations, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $6.3B. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction lead industries with $6.9B and $6.6B respectively. RAYTHEON COMPANY and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP are the top vendors by obligations, with $9.7B and $5.6B. This mix of defense, homeland security, construction, and health-related spending shapes federal contracting opportunities in the state through the current fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Arizona changed over recent years?",
        "body": "Arizona obligations rose to $29.3B in FY26 from $23.3B in FY25, representing an increase year over year of up 25.5%. Obligations varied earlier in the decade, with totals of $16.5B in FY21, $15.3B in FY22, $19.8B in FY23, and $18B in FY24. The current fiscal year totals are partial, fiscal year to date. The number of awards in FY26 to date is 18,502 with an average award size of $1.6M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers and industries for federal contracts in Arizona?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $20.9B in obligations, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $6.3B and the General Services Administration with $625M. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligations at $6.9B, and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction follows with $6.6B. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers account for $5.6B in obligations, reflecting significant health-related contract volume. Aircraft Manufacturing and research services also feature, with $2.6B and $2.1B respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much federal contract money goes to Arizona?",
      "a": "Arizona receives $29.3B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state has 18,502 awards so far and the average award size is $1.6M. Obligations are up 25.5% year over year, rising from $23.3B in FY25 to $29.3B in FY26. Historical yearly totals include $16.5B in FY21, $15.3B in FY22, $19.8B in FY23, and $18B in FY24."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies award the most contracts in Arizona?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense awards the most contracts in Arizona, obligating $20.9B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security is the second-largest buyer with $6.3B, and the General Services Administration follows with $625M. Other notable buyers include the Department of Veterans Affairs with $429M and the Department of the Interior with $319M. Together the top buyers account for the bulk of FY26 obligations."
